---

The profiler answers one question: **why is this server render slow?** It names the slow components, functions, network calls, and allocations. There is no per-component instrumentation, and nothing to wrap by hand.

You configure it fully in the Vite plugin (`ogygia({ profiler: true })`). `ogygia.handle()` mounts it automatically. There is no separate hook to add, and no route files to write.

## The idea

When a page takes seconds to render on the server, the cause is rarely visible from the outside. It can be a heavy component, a `load` function that awaits three services one after another, a markdown pass, or the garbage collector. A CPU profiler alone misses the waits. A request log alone misses the compute.

Thus the profiler watches the **whole Node process** during a render, and splits the wall clock into everything that occurred:

- component SSR and `load` functions,
- outbound `fetch` / `http` calls, tied to the route that made them,
- garbage collection and memory growth,
- and time spent in a **wait**, not in compute.

One trick makes it readable. Svelte compiles each component to a function **named after its file**. Thus a V8 sample taken inside a render already knows that it is in `Cart` or `ProductGrid`. The profiler reads those names directly off the stack. You never wrap a component to measure it.

## Install

Set it on in `vite.config.ts` — nowhere else. `ogygia.handle()` mounts it for you. Thus `src/hooks.server.ts` stays a plain `sequence(...)`:

```ts
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';

export default defineConfig({
	plugins: [ogygia({ profiler: true }), sveltekit()]
});
```

Pass options in place of `true` to adjust it: `ogygia({ profiler: { secret, path, sampleInterval } })`. In production, set a secret. Then the UI is reachable and gated:

```bash
OGYGIA_PROFILER_SECRET=some-long-random-string
```

Then open **`/__profiler`**. In development, the UI is open. In production, it 404s until you **log in** with the secret. A session cookie holds the login, so the key never rides in a URL. It is also reachable over MCP: `ogygia mcp` / `ogygia ai` give the profiler to an AI agent. The agent can run a profile, or read a downloaded report with no live server.

## What you get

- **Always on, near zero cost.** Each request is wall-timed and logged, with per-route p50/p95. The CPU is sampled only while you record.
- **On demand.** Profile a page by URL, or profile a single request with a header. See [Recording](/docs/profiler/recording).
- **A readable report.** A wall-clock budget bar (compute against waits). An interactive treemap of where the CPU went. Sortable component and function tables, with per-call cost and `×N` repeat counts. A network waterfall: each request expands to its formatted JSON payload, and its wire and decoded sizes. A waiting-by-function breakdown for non-HTTP I/O. Memory and GC. A flame graph. See [Reading a report](/docs/profiler/reading-reports).
- **Share it, or open it in the devtools.** A report exports as an encrypted `.ogp`. You open it again through Import. That is the serverless answer, where a report cannot stay in memory. If you also set [devtools](/docs/devtools/overview) on, its Profiler tab runs the same page profiles inline.

## What it needs

A **Node.js server**: `adapter-node`, `vite preview`, or most Node hosts. It uses the built-in V8 inspector. On edge runtimes without the inspector (Cloudflare Workers, Deno Deploy), the always-on request log still operates. CPU and heap recording do not. The UI says so plainly. On serverless Node hosts (Lambda, Amplify), recording operates, but the instance is short-lived. Download the report as a dump and view it anywhere. See [Serverless and edge](/docs/profiler/recording).

It is production-safe. The UI 404s without the secret. The match is timing-safe. Recorded profiles live only in memory, never on disk.
